For a Doubly Linked List, design an algorithm that reverses the list in-place so that the first node 
becomes the last, the second node becomes the second last, and so on. Return the new head of the 
reversed list. Your pseudocode should clearly show how you handle the prev and next pointers for each 
node and how you update the head of the list after reversal. 
Note: An in-place algorithm modifies the data structure directly without using extra space proportional to 
the input size. For a doubly linked list, this means: 
• 
No new list is created. 
• 
No auxiliary arrays or stacks. 

struct Node *reverse(struct Node *curr) { 

 // Base case: If the list is empty or we 

// reach the end of the list 

 if (curr == NULL) return NULL; 

 // Swap the next and prev pointers 
 struct Node *temp = curr->prev; 
 curr->prev = curr->next; 
 curr->next = temp; 

 // If the previous node (after swap) is NULL 

// his is the new head 
 if (curr->prev == NULL) return curr; 

 return reverse(curr->prev); 
}

